A template is the right choice when the website's job is to exist and be found: your hours, your location, a phone number, a few photos, one or two services. A custom build earns its money when the website is doing the selling, when your content has a shape no template was designed for, or when speed and search are actually bringing you customers. I build custom websites for a living and I still send people to a template roughly as often as not, because getting this wrong is expensive in both directions. Here is where the line actually sits, with real numbers on both sides.

## Do You Need a Custom Website or a Template?

Work from what the site has to do, not from what it costs.

**A template is enough when:**

- The site is under five pages and the content is standard: about, services, contact
- Nobody in your market competes seriously on search
- You update it yourself, occasionally, and want to keep doing that
- Your budget genuinely stops under a few hundred
- You need something live this week

**A custom build pays for itself when:**

- The site has to convert rather than inform, and you can name the action: a booking, an enquiry, an order
- Your content has its own structure, such as a menu in two languages, a property catalogue, or programmes with dates and prices
- You compete on search terms that bring in money
- Speed matters because your audience is on phones with patchy signal
- You want to own the thing rather than rent it

The question is not which one is better. It is whether your website is a business card or a salesperson. A business card does not need custom engineering, and a salesperson is worth paying for.

## What Does Each One Actually Cost Over Three Years?

This is where most comparisons quietly cheat, by putting a monthly subscription next to a one-time build and calling the subscription cheaper. Compare them over the same window.

**The template route.** Checked today, Wix runs $17.77 to $39.77 a month for its business plans, billed annually, and Squarespace sits between $12 and $36 a month depending on plan and billing. Over three years that is roughly $450 at the cheap end and $1,400 at the top, before a premium template, before paid apps or plugins, and before the domain. The item nobody prices is your own time: a weekend of dragging boxes around, then an evening every time something needs changing.

**The custom route.** My own numbers: $500 to $1,000 for a one-page site and $1,000 to $2,000 for a 3 to 5 page business website, once. Hosting a site that size on Vercel or Netlify is free or close to it, and a domain is ten to fifteen dollars a year. I break down what moves that number in my guide to [what a custom site actually costs](/blog/web-developer-cost/).

Put those side by side over thirty six months and they land closer together than either camp admits. A template is not automatically cheaper, and a custom build is not automatically an indulgence. What genuinely differs is what you hold at the end of the three years, and what happens the day you want to change something the template was not built to do.

That day is worth picturing, because it is the real cost and it never appears in a pricing table. You want the three featured rooms on the homepage to come from the same place as the rooms page, so a price change happens once. The template has no concept of a room. So you either pay for an app that half solves it, or you keep two lists in your head and update both, or you redesign around the limitation. None of those three show up as a line item, and all of them cost more than the subscription.

## Can a Template Website Rank on Google?

Yes, it can. Most articles telling you otherwise are written by people who sell custom development, so take mine with the same suspicion and read Google instead.

Google's [SEO starter guide](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/seo-starter-guide) puts it plainly: creating content that people find compelling and useful will likely influence your website's presence in search results, and Google calls that more influential than the technical suggestions in the same document. Nothing in that guide ranks one platform above another. Your builder is not a ranking factor.

Where the platform does bite is speed. Google's [page experience documentation](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/page-experience) states that Core Web Vitals are used by its ranking systems, while also being clear that there is no single signal doing the work.

So the honest mechanism is this: a template site is not slow because it is a template. It is slow because of what accumulates on top of one. Unoptimised images at full camera resolution. Four plugins doing the job of one. A theme shipping code for a shop and a forum you never enabled.

That is measurable rather than theoretical. [Casera](/blog/casera/) came to me loading twenty to thirty unoptimised photographs and taking five to eight seconds on a phone. The rebuilt site loads in about two seconds with the same photography, because the images go through a pipeline instead of straight from a camera roll.

There is a second way template sites lose search traffic, and it is quieter. Themes generate pages you never asked for: tag archives, category listings, author pages, paginated duplicates of the same six posts. Google crawls all of it, finds very little worth keeping, and learns that most of what your site publishes is thin. A build with five deliberate pages and nothing else gets judged on five pages. That is not a platform rule, it is a housekeeping one, and you can fix it on a template if you know to look.

## What Can a Custom Build Do That a Template Cannot?

Concrete things, not adjectives. Each one of these is a live site you can open.

**Content shaped like your business.** [Porta Brzi Brod](/blog/porta-brzi-brod/) keeps the menu, prices, opening hours and both languages as structured data. One edit updates the page, the schema markup and the Serbian and English versions at the same moment. On a template that is four separate edits in four places, and they drift apart within a month.

**An image pipeline built for your photos.** [Zvonačka Banja](/blog/zvonacka-banja/) is the kind of site templates handle worst, a tourism page that lives on large photographs. Sizing, format conversion and lazy loading are decisions made once in the build rather than fought with per image.

**Schema markup that matches reality** instead of whatever your theme happens to emit, which matters now that AI assistants read structured data to decide what a business actually offers.

**A site you own.** Export a template site and you get HTML that does not run anywhere else. Leave a custom build and you take the repository, and any developer can pick it up.

**A design your competitor is not also using.** W3Techs put Wix at 4.2 percent and Squarespace at 2.4 percent of all websites on 20 October 2026. That is millions of sites drawing on a few hundred layouts, and in a small market the odds of meeting your own homepage on a competitor's domain are not small.

## Are AI Website Builders Good Enough Now?

They have changed the cheap end of this decision, so the fair answer is: for some jobs, yes.

They genuinely work for a first landing page, for testing whether an idea has a market, for a one-off campaign page, and for anything where being live today beats everything else. Describing what you want and getting a reasonable page back is real, and pretending otherwise is just defensive.

The limits are specific rather than scary. Migrating a site that already ranks is where people get hurt, because URLs change, redirects break and schema markup disappears, and the rankings go with them. Content with real structure behind it is still beyond them. Accessibility tends to lose to visual output. And the result lives inside one platform, which is fine until the day it is not.

The honest summary is that an AI builder gives you a good first draft of a website and a poor final one. That is roughly where templates themselves were ten years ago, and the same advice applies: fine to start on, plan to outgrow it.

## When Is a Template the Right Choice?

This is against my own commercial interest, which is exactly why it is worth writing down. I turn these projects away and point people at a template instead.

- **A brand new business testing whether a market exists.** Spend the money finding customers, not building the thing that displays them a phone number.
- **A side project or a single event.** A site with an expiry date does not deserve a build.
- **A business that gets every customer from Instagram or word of mouth** and needs an address on the internet for the people who look you up afterwards.
- **A budget that genuinely stops at a few hundred.** A cheap custom site is worse than a good template, because corners get cut where you cannot see them.
- **Someone who wants to do it themselves.** If you enjoy the Sunday afternoon of dragging boxes around, a template is a better product for you than I am.

A nine hundred dollar site that exists to show opening hours is a bad use of your money, and I would rather say so than take the project.

## How Do You Decide for Your Own Project?

Five steps, and they work whichever way you lean.

**Write down what the site has to make happen.** A booking, an enquiry, an order, or simply being findable when somebody types your name. Everything else follows from that sentence.

**Check whether your content has a shape.** Count how many places a single change has to be made. One place is template territory. Three places, or two languages, is not.

**Price both over three years, not over month one.** Subscription times thirty six, against a one-time build plus a domain.

**Find out whether anyone searches for what you sell.** If they do, speed and structure are worth paying for. If nobody searches, a beautiful fast site will not create demand that was never there.

**When you are unsure, take the reversible option.** That usually means starting on a template and rebuilding once the site is earning. If you go that way, keep your own domain and write your own copy, because those two things carry across and nothing else does. My guide on [how long the build takes](/blog/how-long-to-build-a-website/) covers what the rebuild looks like, and the comparison of [who should build it](/blog/freelance-developer-vs-agency/) covers who you hand it to.

## Still Weighing It Up?

If your website is a business card, buy a template and spend the difference on getting customers. If it is the thing that turns a stranger into a booking, it is worth building properly, and the gap in cost over three years is smaller than it looks from month one.

If you are not sure which one you have, [tell me what the site has to do](/contact/) and I will tell you straight, including when the answer is a template and not me. You can also look through [my work and how I approach projects](/) to see what the custom route actually produces.
